`useEffect` synchronizes a component with an external system: a subscription, timer, browser API, or network request. React runs it after a render is committed. When dependencies change, React runs the old cleanup before the new setup. It also cleans up on unmount. [React reference](https://react.dev/reference/react/useEffect)

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const connection = connect(roomId);
  return () => connection.disconnect();
}, [roomId]);
```

- **Dependencies:** No array runs after every commit. `[]` runs on mount. `[roomId]` also reruns when `roomId` changes. Include every prop, state value, and component variable the effect reads.
- **Common mistakes:** Missing dependencies cause stale values. Missing cleanup leaves listeners, timers, or connections active. Newly created objects or functions can trigger needless reruns. State updates that change an effectΓÇÖs dependencies can cause loops.
- **Best practice:** Use effects for external synchronization. Calculate display values during render; handle user actions in event handlers. Clean up async work or ignore stale responses to prevent request races. [React guidance](https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect)
- **Development surprise:** `StrictMode` runs an extra setup and cleanup cycle to expose broken cleanup. Fix the cleanup rather than assuming the effect runs only once. [React reference](https://react.dev/reference/react/useEffect)
